UNIT hunk_lines
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@9b9e5d24ca9d cli/lsp/pr-review.mjs hunkLines(diff), the pinned authority; its MAX_HUNK constant and hunkLines are byte-identical to rederive@65149601a522, which carries the architect's round-2 rulings (N1, N2 fixed; N3, N4 kept). Cross-checked against git itself (git diff -U0 --no-index, 5000 random file pairs, anchor/git-anchor.mjs: 5000/5000 agree)
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG hunk_lines(diff: text) -> list[i64]
ENVELOPE any text, typically the output of `git diff -U0` for one file; every magnitude of start and count is in scope
  (R5 bounds what one header can cover by MAX_HUNK). OUT: non-text input, which throws (ruling F5: declared, not
  representable under the SIG).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION From the text of a unified diff, the new-side line numbers its hunk headers cover, ascending, each once. A
  header "@@ -a[,b] +c[,d] @@" covers new-side lines c through c + d - 1. The review system posts inline PR notes only
  on these lines (GitHub accepts line comments only inside the diff), so a wrong answer drops or misplaces a note. The
  function reads ONLY headers; it never looks at the +/- body lines. In git's own output every content line begins with
  "+", "-", " " or a backslash, so only git's real hunk headers can match (the anchor's 5000/5000).
NOTE rust omitted — domain: the review system ships as JavaScript (rdv review and the rederive/sir-review GitHub Action); there is no Rust consumer.
BEHAVIOR
R1 The result is the ascending list, without repeats, of every line number covered (R5) by any header (R3) in diff.
  Headers may arrive in any order and may overlap; the union is sorted numerically. No header -> the empty list. The
  function never throws on in-envelope text.
R2 diff is cut into LINES at each LINE_BREAK character and only there; a line starts at offset 0 or right after a LINE
  FEED. CR, U+2028 LINE SEPARATOR, U+2029 PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR, U+0085 NEXT LINE and every other character are ordinary
  characters inside a line, so header-shaped text after any of them never starts a header. In a CR LF diff the CR stays
  at the end of the earlier line, after that line's header, where R3 ignores it. CITED ruling F1 (2026-11-02)
R3 A header is a line that BEGINS with HEADER. Only that prefix must match: whatever follows the closing " @@" in the
  same line (git's function-context text, a CR, a third "@", further header-shaped text) is ignored and never yields a
  second header. Every line of the whole text is examined, not only lines between hunks.
R4 Numbers are runs of the ten ASCII digits only; leading zeros are allowed and a run of any length is read as its
  exact decimal value. Let C be the new-side start and N the new-side count (TABLE COUNT). The old side "-a[,b]" must
  have the shape, but its values are never read or bounded. CITED ruling F6 (kept)
R5 Let E = C + N - 1, the header's last line, in exact integer arithmetic on the values of R4. The header covers the
  lines L with max(C, 1) <= L <= the upper bound TABLE RANGE gives, or nothing when the table drops it. Hence N = 0
  covers nothing; line 0 is never covered, and a start of 0 covers lines 1 through N - 1 (ruling N3, kept: git never
  writes it); line SAFE_LIMIT itself can be covered. CITED ruling F2 F3 N1 N2 (2026-11-02)
R6 Any line that does not begin with HEADER contributes nothing; there is no partial or lenient reading. In particular
  each separator is exactly one ordinary space (a tab or a doubled space breaks the header); there is no sign; a comma
  must be followed by at least one digit (",": not a header, NOT the default count); a second ",digits" on either side
  breaks the header; "@@@" combined-diff headers are not headers. CITED ruling F4 (kept)
INVARIANT the result is strictly ascending, every element lies in 1 .. SAFE_LIMIT, one header contributes at most
  MAX_HUNK lines, and the result is empty when no line of diff begins with "@@ -".
TABLE COUNT — the new-side text after the start digits, inside HEADER -> N
  absent (the start digits are followed directly by " @@") -> 1
  "," then digits whose value is 0 (",0", ",000")          -> 0
  "," then digits with value d >= 1 (",3", ",0003")         -> d
TABLE RANGE — first row that applies -> the upper bound of R5
  N > MAX_HUNK                    -> dropped
  E <= SAFE_LIMIT                 -> E
  E = SAFE_LIMIT + 1              -> SAFE_LIMIT (the range is CUT one line short, not dropped) CITED ruling N4 (kept, declared)
  E > SAFE_LIMIT + 1              -> dropped
PINNED grammar HEADER = "@@ -" DIGIT+ [ "," DIGIT+ ] " +" DIGIT+ [ "," DIGIT+ ] " @@" CITED R3 R4 — the pr-review.mjs hunkLines pattern; DIGIT = the ASCII characters 0 through 9 only (no fullwidth, no other script's digits); the separators are the ordinary space character U+0020, one each; the first DIGIT+ after " +" is C, the optional second is N
PINNED charset LINE_BREAK = { U+000A LINE FEED } CITED R2 — ruling F1; CR, U+2028, U+2029 and U+0085 are NOT in it
PINNED integer SAFE_LIMIT = 9007199254740991 CITED R5 — 2^53 - 1, the largest integer an IEEE-754 double holds with every smaller integer; rulings F2, N1
PINNED integer MAX_HUNK = 1000000 CITED R5 — the authority's MAX_HUNK; ruling N2: a longer range is dropped, never walked; N = MAX_HUNK itself is KEPT (the oracle carries only the dropped side, ruling N5)
ORACLE value -> oracles/hunk_lines.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 9b3d87c6d5c600ebde832488b091cf07fd93b885effbd12ff52c0b3c24ac6dbe
SCOPE IN: R1–R6 over text. OUT: anything about the diff body (the +/- lines are never read), the git call
  (changedLines in the same module), and non-text input (ENVELOPE).
PROVENANCE rederive@9b9e5d24ca9d (MIT), cli/lsp/pr-review.mjs, export hunkLines; selfhost seam seams/hunk_lines.mjs. Cut
  cold 2026-11-02 on c509956a8f8a (F1–F6), re-cut on 9e2245be5f42 (rulings F1–F6) and on 65149601a522 -> 9b9e5d24ca9d (rulings N1–N6); independent anchor:
  git (anchor/git-anchor.mjs).
